.TH H264\-SORT 1 "2026-10-27" "h264-sort" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
h264\-sort \- move H.264/AVC videos into an h.264 subdirectory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B h264\-sort
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIDIR\fR|\fIFILE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B h264\-sort
examines the video files in the given directories and moves every file whose
first video stream is H.264/AVC into a subdirectory of the same directory,
named
.B h.264
by default. Files with any other codec (HEVC, AV1, MPEG\-2, ...) are left where
they are.
.P
The codec is determined with
.BR ffprobe (1)
from the first video stream, not from the file name or extension, so a
.B .mkv
that actually contains H.264 is detected correctly and a mislabelled container
is not mistaken for H.264.
.P
Every candidate file is probed on every run and no result is remembered
between runs. An earlier version probed one file per capture model and cached
the answer, on the assumption that a model always uses the same codec; that
assumption is false, because a model can be re\-encoded part\-way through its
catalogue. In practice it moved HEVC files into
.B h.264
and left H.264 files behind in the same folder, so the cache has been removed.
A scan of a few hundred TS files over a network mount takes tens of seconds
with the default parallelism; raise
.B \-j
for a cold filesystem.
.P
Files are moved, never copied, and an existing destination file is never
overwritten: a numeric suffix
.RI ( name.1.ext )
is used instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-codec " CODEC"
Collect files whose first video stream is
.I CODEC
as reported by
.BR ffprobe (1)
(default:
.BR h264 ).
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-target " NAME"
Use
.I NAME
as the destination subdirectory instead of
.BR h.264 .
The name defaults to
.B h.264
when the codec is
.BR h264 ,
and to the codec name otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Also search subdirectories. Each directory that contains matches gets its own
target subdirectory; the target directories themselves are pruned and never
searched.
.TP
.BI \-j ", " \-\-jobs " N"
Probe
.I N
files concurrently (default: 8). Lower it for a flaky network mount, raise it
for a local disk.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be moved without changing anything on disk.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS Candidate files
Only these extensions are considered, case\-insensitively:
.BR mkv ,
.BR mp4 ,
.BR mov ,
.BR m4v ,
.BR avi ,
.BR ts ,
.BR mts ,
.BR m2ts ,
.BR webm ,
.BR flv ,
.BR wmv ,
.BR mpg ,
.BR mpeg ,
.BR ogv ,
.BR 3gp .
Without
.B \-r
only the top level of each directory is scanned.
.SS Detection
Each candidate is run through
.BR ffprobe (1)
with
.BR \-select_streams " v:0"
and
.BR \-show_entries " stream=codec_name" .
Files with no video stream and files ffprobe cannot read are reported as
unreadable, counted, and left untouched; so are files whose codec does not
match.
.P
Probing finishes before the first move, so interrupting the scan with
.B Ctrl\-C
leaves the directory exactly as it was.
.SS Requirements
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ffprobe (1)
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview what would be moved in a media folder:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-n /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Move the H.264 files, leaving HEVC where it is:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Handle a folder tree, including nested captures:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-r /mnt/data_storage/Good
.EE
.P
On a local disk, probe sixteen files at a time:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-j 16 /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Gather HEVC files instead, into a folder named
.BR hevc :
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-c hevc \-t hevc \-r /mnt/data_storage/Good
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No move failed. Skipped and unreadable files do not count as failures.
.TP
.B 1
At least one move failed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage (no directory or file given, invalid
.BR \-j ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ts2mkv (1),
.BR ffmpeg\-encode (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for local use.
